[
    {
        "content": "<p>In <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/31908\">#31908</a> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"387244\">@Yaël Dillies</span> introduced the measure <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Probability/Distributions/SetBernoulli.html#ProbabilityTheory.setBernoulli\">ProbabilityTheory.setBernoulli</a> along with a notation <code>setBer(u, p)</code>. Then in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/28248\">#28248</a> they introduced <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Probability/Distributions/Binomial.html#ProbabilityTheory.binomial\">ProbabilityTheory.binomial</a> with two notations: <code>Bin(n, p)</code> and <code>Bin(R, n, p)</code>. It is kind of an issue that the binomial measure is a measure over <code>Nat</code>, and you want to see it like this sometimes, but to exploit some measure-theoretic objects such as the expectation or the characteristic function you need to see it as a measure over <code>Real</code> instead (or maybe over <code>ENat</code> or <code>ENNReal</code> in other contexts). The solution to avoid having one definition for each type is to take the pushforward by <code>Nat.cast : Nat -&gt; R</code> where <code>R</code> is an <code>AddMonoidWithOne</code>. Yaël thus defined <code>Bin(n, p)</code> as a notation for <code>binomial n p</code> and then <code>Bin(R, n, p)</code> for <code>Bin(n, p).map Nat.cast</code>. In <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/37736\">#37736</a> I add some API for the binomial measure and I prove all lemmas for <code>Bin(n, p)</code> and <code>Bin(R, n, p)</code> leading to some duplications but I would say it is quite straightforward (and I don't see how to avoid it).</p>\n<p>Anyway the point is that I really enjoyed these notations, which avoid writing the full name everywhere and are also close to what we write on paper. I thus encouraged <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"970148\">@huaizhangchu</span> to take a similar approach in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/34435\">#34435</a> about the Poisson distribution. However there <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"572064\">@Peter Pfaffelhuber</span> emit <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/34435#discussion_r3060157406\">some reserve</a> about the notation, pointing in particular to the fact that it does not use parentheses the way we usually do in lean. This echoes <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"310045\">@Eric Wieser</span> 's <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/28248#discussion_r2718647012\">comment</a> in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/28248\">#28248</a>. I personally think these notations are great, and regarding parentheses, I was puzzled at first by the notation <code>C(X, Y)</code> for <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ContinuousMap#doc\">docs#ContinuousMap</a> but I now find it great and not particularly disturbing. Also writing the pushforward measure explicitly without a notation gets quite verbose, so that I think it's really worthwile having this notation.</p>\n<p>I even consider that it might be interesting to introduce a notation for every specific measure we have, <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ProbabilityTheory.gaussianReal#doc\">docs#ProbabilityTheory.gaussianReal</a> for instance, even though we don't really have this cast issue here. Maybe these could be scoped further than just in <code>ProbabilityTheory</code>, maybe in <code>ProbabilityTheory.Distributions</code>, but that's not the primary question here.</p>\n<p>Anyway I am writing here to seek more opinion on these notation questions. I personally don't think they do any harm but I am not sure what really are the rules on introducing new notations, so seeing some reserve I prefer to ask here. Also tagging <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"246273\">@Bhavik Mehta</span> who merged <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/28248\">#28248</a>.</p>",
        "id": 584607135,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1775805762
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I should also point out that, in the original version of <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/28248\">#28248</a>, I was adding a semiring-polymorphic binomial measure (so that <code>Bin(n, p)</code> was literally <code>Bin(Nat, n, p)</code>). Bhavik pushed back against it in review on the basis that eg doc#Finset.card is <code>Nat</code>-valued.</p>",
        "id": 584607722,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1775805988
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks for providing more context! For me it is hard to judge about new notation. I see that <code>C(X, Y)</code> is close to what is written on paper, but there is nothing comparable for bounded, or measurable functions (which would appear frequently in my work). As for probability distributions, my question would rather be where to stop introducing new notation, since there are many probability distributions. Let me also mention that -- for <code>PMF</code>, since it is a functor/monad -- writing <code>PMF R := Nat.cast &lt;$&gt; PMF.binomial p _ n</code> would work (i.e. I want to say that it is worth having (discrete) probability distributions as a monad/functor), which is also pretty close what I would write on paper as an image measure. However, <code>Bin(R, n, p)</code> is even shorter...</p>",
        "id": 584614031,
        "sender_full_name": "Peter Pfaffelhuber",
        "timestamp": 1775808165
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If using parenthesis is not desired, one could consider slightly different characters, e.g.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">Bin</span><span class=\"bp\">⟬</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">⟭</span>\n<span class=\"n\">Bin</span><span class=\"bp\">⟮</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">⟯</span>\n<span class=\"n\">Bin</span><span class=\"bp\">⦅</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">⦆</span>\n<span class=\"n\">Bin</span><span class=\"bp\">⦇</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">⦈</span>\n<span class=\"n\">Bin</span><span class=\"bp\">⦉</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">⦊</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 584644596,
        "sender_full_name": "Adomas Baliuka",
        "timestamp": 1775817952
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I thought the consensus was that we <em>didn't</em> like <code>C(X, Y)</code>? And that we'd rather be writing <code>X →\\_c Y</code> if only Unicode had a subscript c.</p>",
        "id": 585300362,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1776124536
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks for stepping in, <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"459227\">@Violeta Hernández</span>. I didn't find a discussion around the notation for <code>C(X, Y)</code> here in zulip, but after some more thoughts I think that the reasons for notation local for some mathematical field (like <code>Bin(n, p)</code>) must be very strong in terms of usage.</p>",
        "id": 585348886,
        "sender_full_name": "Peter Pfaffelhuber",
        "timestamp": 1776151626
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Some time ago I tried to make mathlib's notations for bundled functions more consistent, but I failed, because the notation we wanted was a subscript <code>c</code> to indicate continuity. And people were not so happy with the superscript alternative.</p>",
        "id": 585360956,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1776155334
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>But is the issue just about consistency with for instance <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=BoundedContinuousFunction#doc\">docs#BoundedContinuousFunction</a>, or is it that bad to have that kind of parentheses in any notation? I feel like it should not be that bad.</p>",
        "id": 585362225,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1776155677
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I agree that it can be useful sometimes to have a notation with brackets. But <code>C(X, Y)</code> notation is so unlike any other bundled function in Mathlib that it is really confusing.</p>",
        "id": 585363281,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1776155951
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>We have <code>A →L[k] B</code> and <code>A →ᵃ[k] B</code>. I don't see why we can't have <code>A →ᶜ B</code></p>",
        "id": 585404475,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1776167886
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The previous discussion is at <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/RFC.3A.20notation.20for.20continuous.20maps.20in.20algebra/with/491900807\">https://leanprover.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/RFC.3A.20notation.20for.20continuous.20maps.20in.20algebra/with/491900807</a></p>\n<p>Feel free to continue there :)</p>",
        "id": 585421856,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1776172218
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Last time I wanted to type <code>C(X, Y)</code>, my first instinct was to type <code>X →ₜ Y</code> because <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Homeomorph#doc\">docs#Homeomorph</a> is <code>X ≃ₜ Y</code>.</p>",
        "id": 585425806,
        "sender_full_name": "Yuyang Zhao",
        "timestamp": 1776173158
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is kind of going off-topic. The initial question was about notations for probability measures such as <code>Bin(n, p)</code> for binomial, <code>Po(r)</code> for Poisson etc. It does not seem to bother anyone so I'll interpret that as the fact people are fine with it.</p>",
        "id": 585427105,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1776173461
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>My take is that it's weird to say \"function application is spelt <code>f x y</code> not <code>f(x, y)</code> in Lean, except in probability theory where actually it is spelt <code>f(x, y)</code>\"</p>",
        "id": 585427559,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1776173574
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"455791\">Yuyang Zhao</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Notation.20for.20specific.20probability.20distributions/near/585425806\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Last time I wanted to type <code>C(X, Y)</code>, my first instinct was to type <code>X →ₜ Y</code> because <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Homeomorph#doc\">docs#Homeomorph</a> is <code>X ≃ₜ Y</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Please continue this discussion in the other thread.</p>",
        "id": 585428234,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1776173733
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"310045\">Eric Wieser</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Notation.20for.20specific.20probability.20distributions/near/585427559\">said</a> :</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>My take is that it's weird to say \"function application is spelt <code>f x y</code> not <code>f(x, y)</code> in Lean, except in probability theory where actually it is spelt <code>f(x, y)</code>\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Well it wouldn't be \"in probability theory\", just in the names of some distributions. My main concern is to make them shorter, especially in the case of casting I explained in the original message. I guess another way would be to have an <code>abbrev</code> <code>Bin R n p := (binomial n p).map Nat.cast</code>? But then should we also have an <code>abbrev</code> <code>Bin n p := binomial n p</code>? That would be weird.</p>",
        "id": 585431023,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1776174414
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Isn't this similar to <code>Measure[mα] α</code> as opposed to <code>Measure α</code>? So, I suggest to have <code>binomial n p</code> \"as usual\", and <code>binomial[R] n p</code> for the <code>Nat.cast</code>.</p>",
        "id": 585463760,
        "sender_full_name": "Peter Pfaffelhuber",
        "timestamp": 1776181742
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Can we just use the coercion mechanism for the nat cast?</p>",
        "id": 585463986,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1776181797
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>There seems to be an issue with identifying the sigma-algebra:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MeasureTheory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ProbabilityTheory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unitInterval</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MeasurableSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">MeasurableSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddMonoidWithOne</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Coe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">coe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">μ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">μ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">cast</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hX</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasLaw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">binomial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hX</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasLaw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bin</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasLaw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">binomial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 585475453,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1776184693
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><code>example (hX : HasLaw X (binomial n p : Measure ℝ) P) : True := sorry</code> works</p>",
        "id": 585477331,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1776185205
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes I saw this but that does not solve the need for shortness</p>",
        "id": 585477518,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1776185276
    },
    {
        "content": "<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hX</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasLaw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">binomial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">m𝓧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊤</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>works. I think the problem is that <code>HasLaw</code> comes with <code>{m𝓧 : MeasurableSpace 𝓧}</code> but not with <code>[m𝓧 : MeasurableSpace 𝓧]</code>. This would require changing  <code>HasLaw</code>, of course. This here works:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasLaw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝓧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">mΩ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MeasurableSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">m𝓧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MeasurableSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝓧</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝓧</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">μ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝓧</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">volume_tac</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"kn\">protected</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">aemeasurable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">AEMeasurable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fun_prop</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"kn\">protected</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">map_eq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">μ</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hX</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">root_</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">HasLaw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">binomial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 585495070,
        "sender_full_name": "Peter Pfaffelhuber",
        "timestamp": 1776190628
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes but <code>HasLaw</code> is defined this way because the sigma-algebra can be inferred from the measure in a normal setup and we want to be able to handle different sigma-algebra on the same type, so we shouldn't change <code>HasLaw</code>.</p>",
        "id": 585495589,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1776190812
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Happy to see that we are on the same page. </p>\n<p>Actually, for discrete measures, your words \"the sigma-algebra can be inferred from the measure in a normal setup\" don't really apply. For a discrete measure (like <code>binomial</code>), I would expect the sigma-algebra to be <code>⊤</code>. However, you frequently want the Borel sigma-algebra instead. Probably for this reason, <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Measure/Dirac.html#MeasureTheory.Measure.dirac\">dirac</a> comes with <code>[MeasurableSpace α]</code> and not with <code>{MeasurableSpace α}</code>.</p>",
        "id": 585497849,
        "sender_full_name": "Peter Pfaffelhuber",
        "timestamp": 1776191538
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"238446\">@Anne Baanen</span>, are we missing a coercion trick here?</p>",
        "id": 585498038,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1776191609
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"572064\">Peter Pfaffelhuber</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Notation.20for.20specific.20probability.20distributions/near/585497849\">said</a> :</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>your words \"the sigma-algebra can be inferred from the measure in a normal setup\" don't really apply</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I think they do, because the definition of <code>HasLaw</code> implies that the measure is defined on <code>m𝓧</code>, and as a measure is always defined over a specific sigma-algebra, <code>m𝓧</code> has to be equal to said sigma-algebra. Also the reason <code>dirac</code> is defined with <code>[MeasurableSpace _]</code> is that you don't want to specify the sigma-algebra each time, because there is no way to infer it from the arguments.</p>",
        "id": 585505984,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1776194259
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"310045\">Eric Wieser</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Notation.20for.20specific.20probability.20distributions/near/585498038\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"238446\">Anne Baanen</span>, are we missing a coercion trick here?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Type-wise, since the right-hand-side <code>Measure R</code> cannot be inferred from the left-hand-side, we should be using <code>Coe</code>, <code>CoeTail</code> or <code>CoeDep</code>. I tried all three and none of them seemed to really work. The problem is Lean manages to infer that we want a <code>@Measure Real ?m_6</code> before it assigns a value to <code>?m_6</code>. And then <code>?m_6</code> is a metavariable at a higher level so it doesn't get assigned when we look for the <code>Coe</code> instance. (For parallelism and predictability reasons, instance synth should happen independent of metavariable assignment, and then only later is it checked for consistency.) But it never does get assigned since <code>HasLaw</code> has it as a plain implicit.</p>",
        "id": 585606758,
        "sender_full_name": "Anne Baanen",
        "timestamp": 1776243916
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Here, all <code>example</code>s compile. Or is this bad for other reasons?</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cas</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Coe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">infer_instance</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">coe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">μ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">μ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">cast</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hX</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasLaw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">binomial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hX</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasLaw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"n\">binomial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hX</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasLaw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bin</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hX</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasLaw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">binomial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 585759807,
        "sender_full_name": "Peter Pfaffelhuber",
        "timestamp": 1776287650
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh, interesting! Looks like the extra <code>inferInstance</code> left in the term causes unification to try to unstuck the <code>?m_6 =?= @inferInstance (MeasurableSpace ℝ) ?m_25</code> goal by finding the <code>Measure ℝ</code> instance we want it to find, instead of there being two metavariables that result in postponing the search to the end. Any reducible identity function works here:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MeasureTheory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ProbabilityTheory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unitInterval</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MeasurableSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MeasurableSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddMonoidWithOne</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">abbrevId</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cas</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Coe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span clas